This first paragraph is the opening. Write it the way the published articles
open: a plain, concrete statement that earns attention without overselling.
The body is Markdoc (a superset of Markdown), so everything below works the
same as in any `.md` file.

## Use H2 for the main sections

Headings start at level 2 (`##`) because the article title is already the H1.
Keep sections short and let each one carry a single idea. Sub-points can go a
level deeper with `###` when a section genuinely needs it.

Paragraphs are just blank-line-separated text. *Italic* and **bold** both work.
Links look like [this](https://mandalacodes.com/universal-language).

### Lists, when a section needs structure

- A bullet for each parallel point.
- Keep them short; if a bullet runs to three lines, it probably wants to be a
  paragraph instead.
- **Lead term.** A bold lead and a short gloss is a clean way to define things,
  matching the house style in the mandala article. (No em dashes anywhere on
  this site: use a comma, a period, a colon, or "to" for ranges.)

Numbered steps work when order matters:

1. First the reader does this.
2. Then this.
3. The payoff lands here.

## Quotes and emphasis

> A blockquote sets apart a line worth pausing on: a definition, a saying, or
> a sentence you want to slow the reader down for.

## Closing

End by pointing somewhere. If the piece relates to a specific card, set
`relatedCard` in the frontmatter above and the footer link to that card renders
automatically. Otherwise close with an invitation, the way the mandala article
ends by inviting the reader to draw a card.

